names: Names in C++

gocpp keeps a Go name in C++ wherever it can. A name gets a _ suffix when C++ reserves it: a
keyword or alternative token (new, class, delete, and), an extension word of g++ or clang++,
a macro of the headers that the runtime includes (EOF, errno, NULL), a name the generated
code uses unqualified (int64_t), or a name that starts with gp_ (the generated names). The
suffix keeps the mapping one to one: new becomes new_ and the user's own new_ becomes new__.
Names that the generated code makes (gp_t0, gp_df, gp_inst_...) start with gp_ and never end
in _, so they cannot clash.

A shadowing variable is declared in a block of its own, so the C++ scoping matches Go's. The
package namespace is gpkg_<path>, with the import path escaped.

Specification: docs/spec/cpp-mapping.md#names-inside-a-package
See also: gocpp explain order
Also known as: naming, escapes, reserved

Example (names.gp):

    package main

    import "fmt"

    func main() {
    	new := 1 // new is a C++ keyword
    	class := 2
    	delete := new + class
    	fmt.Println(new, class, delete)
    }

The C++ that gocpp emit -annotate writes for it:

    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #pragma once
    #include <gocpp/rt.hpp>

    namespace gpkg_main {
    void main();

    } // namespace gpkg_main
    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/names.gp.cpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/names.gp.cpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #include "gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp"

    namespace gpkg_main {

    // ---- ./names.gp:5: func main() {
    void main() {

    // ---- ./names.gp:6: new := 1 // new is a C++ keyword
    // gp[names] col 2: new is written new_ in C++: the name is reserved there (a keyword, a macro of
    //     the headers, or a name the generated code uses)
        int64_t new_ = 1;

    // ---- ./names.gp:7: class := 2
    // gp[names] col 2: class is written class_ in C++: the name is reserved there (a keyword, a macro
    //     of the headers, or a name the generated code uses)
        int64_t class_ = 2;

    // ---- ./names.gp:8: delete := new + class
    // gp[names] col 2: delete is written delete_ in C++: the name is reserved there (a keyword, a macro
    //     of the headers, or a name the generated code uses)
        int64_t delete_ = new_ + class_;

    // ---- ./names.gp:9: fmt.Println(new, class, delete)
    // gp[interfaces] col 2: fmt.Println: the runtime's gp::fmt functions take each operand with the
    //     descriptor of its Go type (gp::fmt::as), not boxed into an interface first (an operand of a
    //     non-pointer type with an Error method is, see the allocation note)
        gp::fmt::Println(new_, class_, delete_);

    // ---- ./names.gp:10: }
    }

    } // namespace gpkg_main
